          %h2#faq FAQ
          %h3#what_is_homotopy_type_theory What is homotopy type theory?
          %h4#for_the_person_on_the_street For the person on the street
          %p Homotopy type theory is a theory of objects, which we call terms, collections of objects, which we call types, and equalities between any two objects in a collection, which we call identity types.
          %p One fundamental aspect of mathematics is that equalities between different collections of objects behave differently from each other. If we have a type where for all terms have exactly one identity between them, this is called a proposition or a truth value. The type with no terms is trivially a proposition, as every single one of the nonexistent terms have exactly one identity between them.
          %p
            However, not all types are propositions: take the type with two distinct terms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
          %p
            Still, not all types are sets: take the type of sets with two terms, the first one with terms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi d
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
            and between
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi d
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d
            and between
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
          %p
            One could continue the process, yielding 2-groupoids whose identity types are groupoids, 3-groupoids whose identity types are 2-groupoids, and so forth. And finally, one could have types whose identity types are general types: these types are also known as
            = succeed "-groupoids." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
          %h4#WhatIsHoTTForSetTheorists For set theorists
          %p
            Homotopy type theory is a refinement of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+set+theory"} constructive set theory
            that takes fully seriously the constructive nature also of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+types"} identity
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive mathematics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axioms"} axioms
            added, this subsumes, rather than excludes, classical mathematics, see below at
            = succeed ".)" do
              %em
                %a{:href => "#IsHoTTLimitedToConstructiveMathematics"} Is HoTT limited to constructive mathematics?
          %p
            It is this insistence on constructive witnesses for identity and on witnesses for identity of such witnesses, and so ever on, that makes what in traditional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set theory
            are just
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
            (whose elements are either equal or not) in homotopy type theory instead be
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoids"} groupoids
            (whose elements may have non-trivial
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
            between them) and indeed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-groupoids"} 2-groupoids
            (with isomorphisms between these isomorphisms) and so ever on; hence what makes what used to be just
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
            be what is kown as
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-groupoids"} ∞-groupoids
            or
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+types"} homotopy types
          %p
            More technically, restriction to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/0-truncated+types"} 0-truncated types
            in HoTT (the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/h-set"}> h-set
            s”) gives a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/predicative+topos"} predicative topos
            “of sets”, and a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            if one allows the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/resizing+axiom"} resizing axiom
            (details are
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "http://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/set%20theory#ReferencesInHomotopyTypeTheory"} here
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
            to HoTT, one obtains a model of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETCS"} ETCS
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CZF"} CZF
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZFC"} ZFC
          %p
            Conversely HoTT has
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/models"} models
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZFC"} ZFC
            (+a number of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+universes"} Grothendieck universes
            to model the type theoretic universes), namely in structures called
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-toposes"} (∞,1)-toposes
            which are presented by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaves"} presheaves
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+sets"} simplicial sets
            = succeed ")" do
              %em
                %a{:href => "#InterpretationInInfinityToposes"} Does HoTT have models in infinity-toposes?
          %p
            The nature and role of these higher toposes in HoTT may be understood by analogy with the familar
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/forcing"} forcing
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/models"} models
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZF"} ZF
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/forcing"} forcing
            extensions. The same is true for
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+set+theory"} constructive set theory
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/law+of+excluded+middle"} law of excluded middle
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/permutation+models"} permutation models
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sites"} sites
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/models"} models
            are called “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"}> 1-toposes
            ”. Now in HoTT we have an even more general sort of forcing appropriate for
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-sites"} (∞,1)-sites
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-toposes"} (∞,1)-toposes
          %h4#for_type_theorists For type theorists
          %p (…)
          %h4#for_homotopy_theorists For homotopy theorists
          %p (…)
          %h3#WhyShouldICare Why should I care?
          %h4#for_the_person_on_the_street_2 For the person on the street
          %p Homotopy type theory is a foundation upon which all of mathematics is based upon. Every collection of objects in mathematics, such as the collection of natural numbers, collection of real numbers, collection of strings of characters in an alphabet, collection of propositions and the collection of all sets, is represented in homotopy type theory. It is also a language in which one could do mathematics in, from algebra to geometry to calculus.
          %h4#WhyShouldICareForSetTheorists For set theorists
          %p
            Traditional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set theory
            is formulated in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first-order+logic"} first-order logic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositions+as+types"} propositions as types
            interpretation, first order logic maps soundly and completely into
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
            (
            %a{:href => "type+theory#MartinLoef74"}> Martin-Löf 74, section 3.1
            ,
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "pure+type+system#Barendregt91"} Barendregt 91, section 4
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+0-types"} homotopy 0-types
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            already give a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+set+theory"} constructive set theory
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/h-set"} h-set
            for more.
          %h4#for_type_theorists_2 For type theorists
          %h4#WhyShouldICareForHomotopyTheorists For homotopy theorists
          %p
            Most homotopy-theoretic theorems that are proven in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Homotopy+Type+Theory+--+Univalent+Foundations+of+Mathematics"} HoTT textbook
            are, in their traditional informal version, material of a first-year course on homotopy theory. Experts who do not care about
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+proof"} formal proof
            might not be impressed yet.
          %p
            But the point is that there is significant prospect. By the discussion at
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a{:href => "#InterpretationInInfinityToposes"} Does HoTT have models in infinity-toposes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+language"} internal language
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-toposes"} (∞,1)-toposes
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
          %p
            Historically, making use of the
            = succeed "-perspective" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"} internal logic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elementary+toposes"} elementary toposes
            led to substantial insight into all theory that uses
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+toposes"} Grothendieck toposes
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+geometry"} algebraic geometry
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacob+Lurie"} Lurie
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higher+Topos+Theory"} Higher Topos Theory
            based on
            = succeed "\u2019s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Charles+Rezk"} Charles Rezk
            %em
              %a{:href => "infinity,1-topos#Rezk10"} Homotopy Topos Theory
            is an astounding piece of work, it falls short of saying anything about this crucial internal aspect of (higher) topos theory. Homotopy type theory is precisely what fills this gap. For more on how this works see at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/HoTT+methods+for+homotopy+theorists"} HoTT methods for homotopy theorists
          %p
            An illustration of the use of this is the proof of the
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Blakers-Massey+theorem"} Blakers-Massey theorem
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9EGrpd"} ∞Grpd
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ≃
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \simeq
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mi whe
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_{whe}
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-toposes"} (∞,1)-toposes
            with an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-site"} (∞,1)-site
            of definition by, essentially, reducing
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stalk"} stalkwise
            to the classical theorem (details are
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "Blakers-Massey%20theorem#Rezk10"} here
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "Blakers-Massey%20theorem#LumsdaineFinsterLicata13"} here
            %em new
            and more elegant proof of Blakers-Massey in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9EGrpd"} ∞Grpd
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ≃
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \simeq
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mi whe
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_{whe}
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-toposes"} (∞,1)-toposes
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-site"} (∞,1)-site
            of definition. This is something that some homotopy theorists tried to find by classical means and failed (details are
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "Blakers-Massey%20theorem#Rezk14"} here
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elementary+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-toposes"} elementary (∞,1)-toposes
          %p
            It seems rather plausible that this is just a simple first example of a future where HoTT methods allow to enter new territory in classical homotopy theory. Of course it will require some expert homotopy theorists to seriously look into the internal-language-of-
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn ∞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            \-toposes way of doing homotopy theory. This is now an open problem for homotopy theorists just as it is for type theorists.
          %h3#WhatRoleDoesTheUnivalenceAxiomPlay What role does the univalence axiom play?
          %h4#for_the_person_on_the_street_3 For the person on the street
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom"} axiom
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalence"} univalence
            may be thought of as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+logic"} formalization
            of what might be called the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principle+of+equivalence"} principle of equivalence
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematics"} mathematics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematical+structures"} mathematical structures
            which are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence"} equivalent
            should behave the same, satisfy the same theorems and so forth.
          %p
            In particular, in elementary
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euclidean+geometry"} Euclidean geometry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isometry"} isometry
            (distance-preserving transformation) between the two shapes. Usually, this congruence requires the need for a quotient of the isomorphism classes in the category of geometric shapes, but with univalence, one does not need such a construction: equality of shapes is the isometries between the shapes.
          %p
            Obvious as this may seem, this principle may be violated in other
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundations+of+mathematics"} foundations of mathematics
            such as
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZFC"} ZFC
          %h4#for_set_theorists_3 For set theorists
          %h4#for_type_theorists_3 For type theorists
          %p
            The axiom of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalence"} univalence
            added to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Martin-L%C3%B6f+type+theory"} Martin-Löf type theory
            implies all of the following:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functional+extensionality"} functional extensionality
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient+types"} quotient types
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+inductive+types"} higher inductive types
                with nontrivial homotopies,
              %p Which in turn imply a wealth of further structure such as (but not limited to)
              %ol
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bracket+types"} bracket types
                %li
                  %p etc.
          %h4#for_homotopy_theorists_3 For homotopy theorists
          %p
            In view of the answer at
            %em
              %a{:href => "#InterpretationInInfinityToposes"} Does HoTT have interpretation in infinity-toposes?
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom"} axiom
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalence"} univalence
            axiomatizes the presence of an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object+classifier"} object classifier
            in an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} (∞,1)-topos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}
            (details are
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "univalence+axiom#GepnerKock12"} here
          %p
            In the default choice
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi H
                  %mo =
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H} =
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9EGrpd"} ∞Grpd
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ≃
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \simeq
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mi whe
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_{whe}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
            of traditonal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            this is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+principal+%E2%88%9E-bundle"} universal fibration
            over the disjoint union of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+spaces"} classifying spaces
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mi B
                  %mi Aut
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \coprod_{[F]} B Aut(F)
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/automorphism+%E2%88%9E-groups"} automorphism ∞-groups
            of all (small)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+types"} homotopy types
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [F]
          %p
            Therefore from the axiom of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalence"} univalence
            follows for instance the theory of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-actions"} ∞-actions
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associated+%E2%88%9E-bundles"} associated ∞-bundles
            and of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+cohomology"} twisted cohomology
            (details are
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/Principal+%E2%88%9E-bundles+--+theory%2C+presentations+and+applications"} here
          %h3#what_advantages_does_homotopy_type_theory_have_over_set_theory What advantages does homotopy type theory have over set theory?
          %p
            As explained at
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a{:href => "#WhatIsHoTTForSetTheorists"} What is HoTT for set theorists?
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structural+set+theory"} structural set theory
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Algebraic+set+theory"}> Algebraic set theory
            ,
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETCS"} ETCS
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/material+set+theory"} material set theory
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZFC"}> ZFC
            ), but moreover it allows us to natively capture
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher categorical
            (more precisely,
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinity-groupoid"} higher groupoidal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopical
            reasoning. Moreover, as a practical foundation, set theory may be compared to the Turing machine model, or perhaps more generously, say,
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ALGOL"} ALGOL
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Coq"} Coq
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Agda"} Agda
          %h3#IsHoTTLimitedToConstructiveMathematics Is homotopy type theory limited to constructive mathematics?
          %p
            No. On the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/0-types"} 0-types
            (the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/h-sets"} h-sets
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+logic"} classical logic
            may be imposed, such as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/law+of+excluded+middle"} law of excluded middle
            (details are
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "excluded+middle#ReferencesInHomotopyTypeTheory"} here
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
            (details are
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "axiom+of+choice#ReferencesInHomotopy"} here
          %p
            One
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model"} model
            that interprets the resulting axioms is the standard model in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+sets"} simplicial sets
            (
            %a{:href => "model%20structure%20on%20simplicial%20sets#KapulkinLumnsdaineVoevodsky12"}> here
            ) inside
            = succeed "+" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZFC"} ZFC
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inaccessible+cardinals"} inaccessible cardinals
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/0-types"} 0-types
            in this model are precisely the ordinary sets in ZFC. See also the discussion at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "#WhatIsHoTTForSetTheorists"} What is HoTT? For set theorists
          %p For more exposition see also
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andrej+Bauer"}> Andrej Bauer
              ,
              %em
                %a{:href => "http://math.andrej.com/2014/01/13/univalent-foundations-subsume-classical-mathematics/"} Univalent foundations subsume classical mathematics
              (2013)
          %h3#InterpretationInInfinityToposes
            Does HoTT have interpretation/semantics/models in
            = succeed "-toposes?" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
          %p
            The short answer is: Yes. Homotopy type theory has
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
            in every
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} (∞,1)-topos
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
            has
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/toposes"} toposes
          %p The more detailed answer depends on which axiomatics precisely one subsumes under “homotopy type theory”. A hierarchy of three main variants (“fragments”) of homotopy type theory should be distinguished here:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                HoTT without
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalence"} univalent
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+universes"} type universes
            %li
              %p
                HoTT with
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalence"} univalent
                weak
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+universes"} type universes
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a{:href => "type%20of%20types#TarskiStyle"} à la Tarski
            %li
              %p
                HoTT with
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalence"} univalent
                strict
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+universes"} type universes
                %a{:href => "type%20of%20types#RussellStyle"} à la Russell
                or
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a{:href => "type%20of%20types#TarskiStyle"} à la Tarski
          %p
            In all three cases the
            = succeed "-categorical" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
            in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type-theoretic+model+category"} type-theoretic model category
            which in turn
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+presentable+%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} presents
            an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-category
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/HoTT+methods+for+homotopy+theorists"} HoTT methods for homotopy theorists
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
            in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+cartesian+closed+category"} locally cartesian closed category
            (see
            %a{:href => "relation+between+type+theory+and+category+theory#DependentTypeTheory"} here
            for details).
          %p With that understood, the higher categorical semantics for the above three cases is as follows:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                HoTT without univalent universes has semantics in every
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+presentable+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} locally presentable
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+cartesian+closed+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} locally cartesian closed (∞,1)-category
                (details are
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a{:href => "locally+cartesian+closed+%28infinity,1%29-category#InternalLogic"} here
            %li
              %p
                HoTT with strict univalent universes has semantics at least in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category+of+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-presheaves"} (∞,1)-presheaf
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-toposes"} (∞,1)-toposes
                over
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elegant+Reedy+categories"} elegant Reedy categories
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/base+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} base (∞,1)-topos
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9EGrpd"} ∞Grpd
                as well as for instance the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sierpinski+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} Sierpinski (∞,1)-topos
                (details are
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a{:href => "relation+between+type+theory+and+category+theory#Shulman13"} here
            %li
              %p
                HoTT with weak univalent universes has semantics in every
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-sheaf+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} (∞,1)-sheaf (∞,1)-topos
                (details are
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/homotopytypetheory/show/model+of+type+theory+in+an+%28infinity%2C1%29-topos"} here
          %p
            Since we are assuming
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+presentable+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} local presentability
            here, all these models have enough
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-colimits"} (∞,1)-colimits
            to interpret
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+inductive+types"} higher inductive types
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+cartesian+closed+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} locally cartesian closed (∞,1)-categories
            which are not necessarily locally presentable.)
          %p
            One may also ask about models in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elementary+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-toposes"} elementary (∞,1)-toposes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elementary+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-toposes"} elementary (∞,1)-toposes
            as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} (∞,1)-categories
            with the minimum of properties such that HoTT may be modeled in them. This reflects the idea that what is “elementary” about elementary
            = succeed "-toposes" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
          %p
            In conclusion, every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proposition"} proposition
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proof"} proven
            in homtopy type theory yields (subject to the above qualification) a statement that holds true in every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} (∞,1)-topos
            (in every presentable
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+cartesian+closed+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} locally cartesian closed (∞,1)-category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+language"} internal language
            of
            = succeed "-toposes." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
          %p
            If one wishes to prove statements that hold only in some class of
            = succeed "-toposes," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axioms"} axioms
            to HoTT that characterize these classes. For instance adding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+modalities"} higher modalities
            that define
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+homotopy+type+theory"} cohesive homotopy type theory
            make it a language that proves theorems which hold in all
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-toposes"} cohesive (∞,1)-toposes
          %h3#what_is_meant_by_a_computational_interpretation_of_univalence What is meant by a “computational interpretation of univalence”?
          %p
            Some type theorists care about
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/canonicity"} canonicity
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terms"} terms
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/types"} types
            be
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/judgmentally+equal"} judgmentally equal
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/canonical+forms"} canonical forms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            or
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s(n)
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            is a canonical form of the natural numbers, and the canonical form of terms in identity types is reflexivity. Canonicity is an important computational trait for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theories"} dependent type theories
            to have as it makes implementing the type theory in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proof+assistant"} proof assistant
            easier.
          %p
            However, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalence+axiom"} univalence axiom
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Martin-L%C3%B6f+type+theory"} Martin-Löf type theory
            causes some terms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+types"} identity types
            to not be
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/judgmentally+equal"} judgmentally equal
            to the canonical form of reflexivity, which breaks canonicity. As a result, some type theorists have looked for alternative dependent type theories which have both canonicity and univalence, such as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cubical+type+theory"} cubical type theory
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+observational+type+theory"} higher observational type theory
          %p
            Alternatively,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vladimir+Voevodsky"} Vladimir Voevodsky
            proposed a variant of canonicity called
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+canonicity"} homotopy canonicity
            which replaces the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/judgmental+equality"} judgmental equality
            of terms to canonical forms with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identifications"} identifications
            of terms with canonical forms: there is an identification between every term of a type and a canonical form, and there is an effective algorithm to compute such identifications (see
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "https://hott.github.io/HoTT-2019/conf-slides/Sattler.pdf"} Sattler 2019
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalence+axiom"} univalence axiom
            is computationally valid since it satisfies
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+canonicity"} homotopy canonicity
          %p
            More generally, many axioms which break canonicity still satisfy homotopy canonicity, such as
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+extensionality"} function extensionality
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/UIP"} UIP
            and typal
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+K"} axiom K
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositional+extensionality"} propositional extensionality
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/descent"} descent
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalence"} univalence
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/excluded+middle"} excluded middle
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
            still break homotopy canonicity, so any computational interpretation of dependent type theory based on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+canonicity"} homotopy canonicity
            remains
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive
          %h3#what_are_higher_inductive_types What are higher inductive types?
          %h3#what_do_we_gain_by_a_proof_in_homotopy_type_theory_of__over_ordinary_proofs
            What do we gain by a proof in homotopy type theory of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi ℤ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_1(S^1) = \mathbb{Z}
            over ordinary proofs?
          %h3#is_it_possible_to_define_higher_coinductive_types Is it possible to define higher coinductive types?
          %p
            Yes, it is currently possible to define higher
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coinductive+types"} coinductive types
            as homotopy-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+coalgebras+for+an+endofunctor"}> terminal coalgebras for an endofunctor
            \. Homotopy-terminal coalgebras for an endofunctor are like the usual notion of terminal coalgebra for an endofunctior, but where equality is expressed via the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+type"} identity type
            instead of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/judgmental+equality"} judgmental equality
            and uniqueness is expressed via
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/contractible+type"} contractibility
            in the definition. However, the resulting higher coinductive types will only have typal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/beta-conversion"} beta-conversion
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/eta-conversion"} eta-conversion
            rules, since only the typal versions are derivable from the universal property of homotopy-terminal coalgebras for an endofunctor as expressed in dependent type theory.
          %h3#in_what_sense_does_homotopy_type_theory_already_contain_logic In what sense does homotopy type theory already contain logic?
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proposition"} proposition
            can be defined as a type such that the identity type between any two terms of the type has a term. One could add to homotopy type theory the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+inductive+type"} higher inductive type
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositional+truncation"} propositional truncation
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product+types"} product types
            correspond to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conjunction"} conjunction
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sum+types"} sum types
            correspond to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/disjunction"} disjunction
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+types"} function types
            correspond to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/implication"} implication
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+product+types"} dependent product types
            correspond to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+quantification"} universal quantification
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+sum+types"} dependent sum types
            correspond to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/existential+quantification"} existential quantification
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/empty+type"} empty type
            corresponds to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/false"} false
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+type"} function type
            into the empty type corresponds to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/negation"} negation
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/contractible+types"} contractible types
            correspond to true. This corresponds to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+logic"} constructive
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/predicate+logic"} predicate logic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/excluded+middle"} excluded middle
            to the type theory yields
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+logic"} classical
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/predicate+logic"} predicate logic
          %h3#can_category_theory_be_carried_out_in_homotopy_type_theory Can category theory be carried out in homotopy type theory?
          %p
            Yes. One could define a
            %strong category
            as a type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            whose identity types
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %msub
                    %mo =
                    %mi C
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a =_C b
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo :
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a:C
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                  %mo :
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b:C
            are sets, with a set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            with functions
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi s
                    %mo :
                    %mi M
                    %mo →
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s:M \to C
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                    %mo :
                    %mi M
                    %mo →
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t:M \to C
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi id
                  %mo :
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} id:C \to M
            and function
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi c
                  %mo :
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                        %mo :
                        %mi M
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                      %mrow
                        %mi g
                        %mo :
                        %mi M
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msub
                      %mo =
                      %mi C
                    %mi s
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo )
                  %mo →
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c:\left(\sum_{f:M} \sum_{g:M} t(f) =_C s(g)\right) \to M
          %p
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi g
                  %mo ,
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msub
                    %mo =
                    %mi C
                  %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s(c(g,f)) =_C s(f)
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi g
                    %mo ,
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msub
                      %mo =
                      %mi C
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t(c(g,f)) =_C t(g)
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:M
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mo ,
                  %mi id
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msub
                    %mo =
                    %mi M
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c(f, id(s(f))) =_M f
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi id
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msub
                      %mo =
                      %mi M
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c(id(t(f)), f) =_M f
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:M
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                    %mo :
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g:M
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi h
                  %mo :
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h:M
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msub
                    %mo =
                    %mi C
                  %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t(f) =_C s(g)
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msub
                      %mo =
                      %mi C
                    %mi s
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi h
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t(g) =_C s(h)
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi h
                    %mo ,
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi g
                    %mo ,
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msub
                      %mo =
                      %mi M
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi h
                    %mo ,
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c(h,c(g,f)) =_M c(c(h,g),f)
          %p
            Then one could define a
            %strong functor
            between categories
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo ,
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (C,M)
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi D
                  %mo ,
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (D,N)
            as a function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo :
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F:C \to D
            with a function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo :
                  %mi M
                  %mo →
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G:M \to N
            such that for all
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:M
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi s
                    %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msub
                    %mo =
                    %mi D
                  %msub
                    %mi s
                    %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(s_C(f)) =_D s_D(G(f))
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi t
                      %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msub
                      %mo =
                      %mi D
                    %msub
                      %mi t
                      %mi D
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(t_C(f)) =_D t_D(G(f))
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo :
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a:C
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi id
                      %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msub
                      %mo =
                      %mi N
                    %msub
                      %mi id
                      %mi D
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G(id_C(a)) =_N id_D(F(a))
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
